## Wet AMD

Wet AMD is treated with **anti-VEGF injections** into the eye, such as **aflibercept**, **ranibizumab** or **faricimab**. They stop new blood vessels growing and leaking.

- your eye is numbed with drops first, so it is not usually painful
- you have injections regularly, often every 1 to 3 months, sometimes for years
- treatment works best when started quickly
- many people keep, or even improve, their sight

## Dry AMD

There is currently no NHS treatment that reverses dry AMD. Some people with a certain level of AMD may be advised to take **eye vitamin supplements** (known as AREDS2), which may slow it down. Ask your eye specialist first.

## Low vision services

A **low vision clinic** can assess your sight and provide:

- **magnifiers** and special lighting
- advice on making the most of your remaining sight
- training in techniques such as **eccentric viewing**, which uses the side of your vision

These are usually free on the NHS.
